<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ionian Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/499-bce">499</a>–493 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ionian Revolt was a rising of the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> and other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> against Persian rule, from 499 to 493 BCE. Led at first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus">Aristagoras of Miletus</a> and supported briefly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria, it ended after the Persian naval victory at Lade and the sack of Miletus in 494 BCE. It led to the Persian invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt.jpg" alt="Ionian Revolt" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting); derivative work by MinisterForBadTimes · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ionian_Revolt_Campaign_Map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Greek settlers from three groups, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolians">Aeolians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a>, emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> after the fall of Mycenaean civilisation. The Ionians founded twelve cities, including Miletus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and Chios, which were independent but shared a common temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panionium">Panionion</a>. They were conquered by the Lydian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> around 560 BCE and then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>'s Achaemenid Empire, which ruled them through native tyrants. In 499 BCE the tyrant of Miletus, Aristagoras, joined the satrap Artaphernes in an expedition against Naxos that failed after a four-month siege. Unable to repay Artaphernes and expecting to lose his position, he chose to incite the Milesians to revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 498 BCE, with twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and five from Eretria, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> took the lower city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, which then burned. Persian troops followed them back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> and routed them. The Athenians withdrew, but the revolt spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, Caria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Three Persian armies set out in 497 BCE to recover the rebel lands. Daurises took several cities on the Hellespont and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carians">Carians</a> at the Marsyas River, though his army was later destroyed in an ambush at Pedasus. Aristagoras left Miletus for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>.</p><p>By 494 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> had regrouped and went straight for Miletus. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> gathered 353 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> at Lade but were decisively beaten after the Samians defected. Miletus was besieged and captured. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> most of its men were killed and its women and children enslaved, which archaeology partly confirms. In 493 BCE the Persians took Chios, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and Tenedos and the remaining cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Artaphernes set a workable peace: disputes between cities were to be settled by arbitration, and tribute was set in proportion to the size of each city's land. The next year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> abolished the tyrannies and put democracies in their place. The Ionian cities recovered quickly, except for Miletus, and could equip a large fleet for the second Persian invasion of Greece 13 years later. The revolt was the first major conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, Darius resolved to punish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria, and the first Persian invasion of Greece followed in 492 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ionian Revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined the <b>Ionian Revolt</b>, and when the revolt collapsed its people fled to Mesembria on the Black Sea, while a Phoenician fleet burned the city in 493 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Ionian Revolt</b> it made the largest contribution of any state to the rebel fleet, sending 100 ships to the Battle of Lade in 494 BCE, where, Herodotus says, its crews fought bravely after others deserted; the Persians ravaged the island the following year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Ionian Revolt</b> and two Persian invasions of Greece, the Allies defeated the forces of Xerxes I at the Battle of Plataea and the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE, then took Sestos and, in 478 BCE, Byzantion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Ionian Revolt</b> the rebels assembled at Ephesus in 498 BCE before marching inland to burn Sardis, but they were defeated near Ephesus on their return, and the city took little further part in the war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its great military venture was the <b>Ionian Revolt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Ionian Revolt</b> Samos sent sixty ships to the Battle of Lade in 494 BCE, but forty-nine of them deserted before the fighting, contributing to the Ionian defeat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Military</a></li></ul></section>
